theory.intro:
The graph of $y = f'(x)$ records the gradient of $y = f(x)$ at every value of $x$. Read the sign of the gradient along the curve, then plot that sign as a height on new axes.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.7,0) -- (3.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.4) -- (0,2.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.2:2.2, smooth, variable=\x] plot (\x, {0.4*(\x*\x*\x - 3*\x)}) node[above left] {$y = f(x)$};
\filldraw (-1,0.8) circle (2pt);
\filldraw (1,-0.8) circle (2pt);
\node[above] at (-1,0.95) {$f'(x) = 0$};
\node[below] at (1,-0.95) {$f'(x) = 0$};
\node at (-2.7,0.55) {$f'(x) > 0$};
\node at (-0.6,-1.1) {$f'(x) < 0$};
\node at (2.7,-0.55) {$f'(x) > 0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

