\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Describe and sketch the graph of the derivative $y = f'(x)$.

development.solution_text:
Since $f(x)$ is decreasing for all $x$, $f'(x) < 0$ everywhere (the derivative lies entirely below the $x$-axis).
As $x \to \infty$, the curve flattens out, so $f'(x) \to 0$ from below.
As $x \to -\infty$, the curve becomes steeper, so $f'(x) \to -\infty$.

[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5) -- (0,1.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-0.3:3.0, smooth] plot (\x, {-2.5*exp(-\x)});
\node[below right] at (1,-0.5) {$y = f'(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

