Describe the nature of the stationary point on $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$.

The graph of $y = f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x - 1}$ is shown below.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5) -- (0,3.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (1,-3.5) -- (1,3.5) node[above] {$x=1$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.5:0.65, smooth] plot (\x, {1/(\x - 1)});
\draw[thick, <->, domain=1.35:4.5, smooth] plot (\x, {1/(\x - 1)});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Sketch $y = f'(x)$ and explain why it lies entirely below the $x$-axis for all $x \neq 1$.

mastery.solution_text:
$f'(x) = -\dfrac{1}{(x - 1)^2}$
Since $(x - 1)^2 > 0$ for all $x \neq 1$, $f'(x) < 0$ on its entire domain.
As $x \to 1$, $f'(x) \to -\infty$; as $x \to \pm\infty$, $f'(x) \to 0$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0) -- (4.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-4) -- (0,1.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (1,-4) -- (1,1.5) node[above] {$x=1$};
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-2.5:0.4, smooth] plot (\x, {-1/((\x - 1)*(\x - 1))});
\draw[thick, <->, domain=1.6:4.5, smooth] plot (\x, {-1/((\x - 1)*(\x - 1))});
\node[below left] at (3.5,-0.5) {$y = f'(x)$};
